---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import '../styles/routes.css';

const title = 'Security · OpenCoven';
const description =
  'How to report OpenCoven vulnerabilities, what security properties are in scope, and where to verify release artifacts.';
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/security">
  <section class="site-shell hero" id="top">
    <div class="hero__copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Security and reporting</p>
      <h1>Report vulnerabilities privately. Verify what you run.</h1>
      <p class="hero__lede">
        OpenCoven handles local agent execution and personal context, so security reports
        should go through private channels—not public issues. Open a repository Security
        Advisory, or join the OpenCoven Discord and privately DM <strong>@BunsDev</strong>.
        Do not post vulnerability details in a public Discord channel.
      </p>
      <div class="button-row">
        <a class="action action--primary" data-oc-primitive="action" data-oc-state="idle" href="https://github.com/OpenCoven/coven/security/advisories/new">Open a private security advisory</a>
        <a class="action" data-oc-primitive="action" data-oc-state="idle" href="https://discord.gg/OpenCoven">Join Discord, then DM @BunsDev</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <aside class="evidence-card" aria-labelledby="security-target-heading">
      <span class="status" data-oc-primitive="status-indicator" data-oc-state="idle">Policy last updated 2026-07-04</span>
      <h2 class="card-heading" id="security-target-heading">Response targets</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        The repository policy states an acknowledgement target of 48 hours and an aim to
        address confirmed vulnerabilities within 14 days. These are project targets, not a
        contractual service-level agreement.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="scope-heading">
    <div class="site-shell">
      <div class="band__intro">
        <h2 id="scope-heading">What belongs in a security report.</h2>
        <p>The current repository policy welcomes reports affecting core routing, memory and session boundaries, authentication and identity handling, execution boundaries, or cross-user and cross-context isolation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-grid">
        <article class="concept-card"><h3>Session isolation</h3><p>One user or agent must not gain another context without explicit permission.</p></article>
        <article class="concept-card"><h3>Identity integrity</h3><p>A familiar identity must not be forgeable merely by another agent or external caller possessing context.</p></article>
        <article class="concept-card"><h3>Execution boundaries</h3><p>Tool and runtime actions must remain within their intended scope and explicit authority boundary.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="verify-heading">
    <div class="site-shell">
      <div class="band__intro">
        <h2 id="verify-heading">Use first-party release evidence.</h2>
        <p>OpenCoven does not claim certifications or independent audits that have not been obtained. Verification should start from repository releases, checksums, signatures, and attestations where the producing repository publishes them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-grid">
        <article class="product-card">
          <h3>Coven Cave releases</h3>
          <p>Inspect the release page and GitHub provenance or attestation evidence associated with the exact artifact you install.</p>
          <a class="action" data-oc-primitive="action" data-oc-state="idle" href="https://github.com/OpenCoven/coven-cave/releases/latest">Open latest Cave release</a>
        </article>
        <article class="product-card">
          <h3>Repository security policies</h3>
          <p>Implementation-specific scope and reporting details remain canonical in each repository's SECURITY.md rather than this public explanation surface.</p>
          <a class="action" data-oc-primitive="action" data-oc-state="idle" href="https://github.com/OpenCoven">Browse OpenCoven repositories</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="nonclaims-heading">
    <div class="site-shell">
      <div class="band__intro">
        <h2 id="nonclaims-heading">Security language stays bounded.</h2>
        <p>Local-first architecture and guarded actions are useful properties, not blanket guarantees.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="architecture">
        <ol>
          <li>No public claim of SOC 2, ISO 27001, third-party audit, formal certification, or complete conformance is made here.</li>
          <li>Third-party dependency and model-provider vulnerabilities should be reported to the relevant upstream provider unless they expose an OpenCoven integration flaw.</li>
          <li>Protocol-level identity and authority guarantees remain maturity-scoped; implemented runtime proof must not be generalized into future cross-device or cloud guarantees.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
